# Golden vectors for the DNS header example.
#
# A query written by `dig` (BIND 9.20.26) and the reply written by the
# resolver on 127.0.0.1, taken in userspace because packet capture is not
# permitted here: a UDP relay sat between the two and recorded both
# datagrams. Neither header was typed.
#
#   dig @127.0.0.1 -p <relay> localhost +tries=1 +noedns
#
# `localhost` on purpose. The local resolver answers it without forwarding,
# so obtaining these bytes put nothing on the network.
#
# The pair is what makes them worth having: the same id in both, the request
# flags against the response flags, and `answer_count` going from zero to
# one. A header is twelve bytes with four counts and two packed bytes, and
# reading one of those bytes backwards is the mistake this catches.
#
# THE QUERY IS WHY THE SCHEMA CHANGED. Its flags are 0x0120, and the AD bit
# dig sets by default lands in what RFC 1035 called the Z field. Committed
# against `reserved u3 [must_be_zero]` it was refused with
#
#     ConstraintError: dns_header.<reserved0> is reserved [must_be_zero]
#
# which was the description being older than the protocol rather than the
# message being wrong. `authentic_data` and `checking_disabled` are named
# fields now and one Z bit remains, so both of these parse and the reserved
# bit is still held to zero -- which both of them satisfy, because neither
# BIND nor the resolver sets it.

dns_header query 03 07 01 20 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00
dns_header reply 03 07 85 80 00 01 00 01 00 00 00 00
